WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.080 --> 00:00:04.910
I'm going to be speaking with David Livingstone
Smith, who's a professor of philosophy at

00:00:04.910 --> 00:00:11.549
the University of New England in Maine and
also author of the book on Inhumanity, Dehumanization

00:00:11.549 --> 00:00:12.940
and How to Resist That.

00:00:12.940 --> 00:00:14.810
I really appreciate your time today.

00:00:14.810 --> 00:00:16.610
Thank you, David.

00:00:16.610 --> 00:00:24.560
So to set the stage for the conversation,
when we talk about dehumanization and inhumanity,

00:00:24.560 --> 00:00:28.449
there are a lot of ways that we could sort
of define those ideas.

00:00:28.449 --> 00:00:31.199
But what what do you mean when you talk about
that?

00:00:31.199 --> 00:00:34.680
Well, dehumanization is a word that's used
in so many different ways.

00:00:34.680 --> 00:00:41.530
I mean, even in the scholarly literature,
what I mean by demonization is the attitude

00:00:41.530 --> 00:00:47.380
of conceiving of other people as subhuman
creatures.

00:00:47.380 --> 00:00:57.100
And in history, there are so many different
examples of when we can say that this dehumanization

00:00:57.100 --> 00:00:58.170
took place.

00:00:58.170 --> 00:01:05.370
There are times where it can take place by
people who are trying to justify horrible

00:01:05.370 --> 00:01:06.600
acts of violence.

00:01:06.600 --> 00:01:12.500
There are times when dehumanization might
even be a coping mechanism by someone exposed

00:01:12.500 --> 00:01:18.640
to extremely harsh or difficult conditions
in some way, shape or form.

00:01:18.640 --> 00:01:23.060
What are some of the different ways that in
human history this has manifested?

00:01:23.060 --> 00:01:26.810
Well, almost always.

00:01:26.810 --> 00:01:29.950
Demonization is the product of propaganda.

00:01:29.950 --> 00:01:34.140
So it's not something that spontaneously rises
in the human mind.

00:01:34.140 --> 00:01:39.920
Rather, it's the result of people in positions
of power who want us to do terrible things

00:01:39.920 --> 00:01:42.070
to one another.

00:01:42.070 --> 00:01:46.350
And the paradigmatic example of that is, is
genocide.

00:01:46.350 --> 00:01:54.229
So if those in power, those who are in positions
of authority, can convince us that these others

00:01:54.229 --> 00:02:02.780
that they wish us to harm are dangerous, toxic,
subhuman creatures that we must destroy in

00:02:02.780 --> 00:02:09.009
our own self defense, then that allows us
to overcome a very natural resistance.

00:02:09.009 --> 00:02:13.030
Human beings have to doing awful things to
one another.

00:02:13.030 --> 00:02:20.700
I'm thinking of so many of the books I've
read about the Holocaust and concentration

00:02:20.700 --> 00:02:31.720
camps and the way in which concentration camp
guards, SS Nazi soldiers, et cetera, by by

00:02:31.720 --> 00:02:32.720
propaganda.

00:02:32.720 --> 00:02:36.770
I mean, quite literally, the story that Hitler
was telling about, about Jews and and about

00:02:36.770 --> 00:02:42.260
some others was about these people are not
really human the way we are.

00:02:42.260 --> 00:02:47.720
But there's also, interestingly, in some of
the memoirs of survivors of concentration

00:02:47.720 --> 00:02:53.790
camps, they said they also experienced the
sort of dehumanization even of their peers,

00:02:53.790 --> 00:02:56.780
which they saw almost as a survival instinct.

00:02:56.780 --> 00:02:59.280
Which which is that a different mechanism?

00:02:59.280 --> 00:03:04.270
Well, yes, we'd have to go into some detail
about what they had in mind.

00:03:04.270 --> 00:03:09.400
But certainly people who are the victims of
dehumanization are quite aware generally that

00:03:09.400 --> 00:03:11.630
they're seen as subhuman creatures.

00:03:11.630 --> 00:03:16.849
I mean, we find this in the example of the
Holocaust and the two examples that I use

00:03:16.849 --> 00:03:25.150
most of my work are the Holocaust, which,
by the way, has centuries old roots in European

00:03:25.150 --> 00:03:26.220
anti-Semitism.

00:03:26.220 --> 00:03:34.270
Jews had been dehumanized since the since
the 12th century in Europe and also the brutal

00:03:34.270 --> 00:03:39.989
oppression of black people in the United States,
particularly in the aftermath of the American

00:03:39.989 --> 00:03:41.340
Civil War.

00:03:41.340 --> 00:03:45.180
And in both those instances, the victims of
demonization.

00:03:45.180 --> 00:03:55.349
We're well aware that they were seen as subhuman
creatures, monsters, demons, vermin.

00:03:55.349 --> 00:04:03.260
As a historical note, what is the is I'm curious
whether you're aware of I know of of antisemitism

00:04:03.260 --> 00:04:05.780
based on the idea of Jews bringing the plague.

00:04:05.780 --> 00:04:10.940
And I'm I don't remember if that's 13th century
or 14th century.

00:04:10.940 --> 00:04:14.680
Is that the earliest example you're aware
of or is there something prior to that?

00:04:14.680 --> 00:04:20.870
Well, well, demonization of Jews, we find
it sporadically earlier.

00:04:20.870 --> 00:04:26.710
So in the Gospel of John, Jews are described
as offspring of Satan.

00:04:26.710 --> 00:04:27.770
Right.

00:04:27.770 --> 00:04:34.530
So that's a very early Christian route of
some anti-Semitic ideas.

00:04:34.530 --> 00:04:35.530
Right.

00:04:35.530 --> 00:04:37.589
So if you're the offspring of Satan, you're
demonic.

00:04:37.589 --> 00:04:38.589
Yes.

00:04:38.589 --> 00:04:40.099
This idea of the demonic Jew persisted.

00:04:40.099 --> 00:04:46.389
But it really took a terrible turn during
the first crusade when Christians marched

00:04:46.389 --> 00:04:50.870
off to to the Holy Land to win it back from
the infidel.

00:04:50.870 --> 00:04:56.150
And along the way, they decided to attack
the infidel in their midst.

00:04:56.150 --> 00:05:01.389
Remember, Jews were the only religious minority
who were permitted to survive in Europe.

00:05:01.389 --> 00:05:02.599
Right.

00:05:02.599 --> 00:05:07.229
And it's from this point onward that Jews
are racialized.

00:05:07.229 --> 00:05:14.210
They're seen as an alien and inferior race
and seen as demonic, devilish, biologically

00:05:14.210 --> 00:05:19.309
different, even though it was believed that
Jewish men menstruated, that Jews had horns

00:05:19.309 --> 00:05:22.050
and tails and and so on.

00:05:22.050 --> 00:05:24.080
And so it just picks up there.

00:05:24.080 --> 00:05:32.189
And then after the bubonic plague of the mid
14th century, it gets really, really, really

00:05:32.189 --> 00:05:41.220
bad when it comes to the I guess we would
the dehumanizes, so to speak.

00:05:41.220 --> 00:05:48.669
Sometimes words or terms like they are monsters,
they are animals, et cetera, are applied,

00:05:48.669 --> 00:05:52.830
which is in a sense is the dehumanization
of the dehumanizes.

00:05:52.830 --> 00:05:55.710
Is is that a is that something we should be
concerned about doing?

00:05:55.710 --> 00:05:57.729
Are the risks of doing that?

00:05:57.729 --> 00:05:58.729
Yeah, absolutely.

00:05:58.729 --> 00:06:00.599
We should be concerned about that.

00:06:00.599 --> 00:06:02.559
Monsters are fictional.

00:06:02.559 --> 00:06:03.559
No one is a monster.

00:06:03.559 --> 00:06:05.430
Hitler was not a monster, I'm Jewish.

00:06:05.430 --> 00:06:08.309
I can say that Hitler was not a monster.

00:06:08.309 --> 00:06:10.059
Goebbels was not a monster.

00:06:10.059 --> 00:06:12.029
These are human beings.

00:06:12.029 --> 00:06:19.800
And it would really benefit us if we considered
them as holding up a mirror to what is possible.

00:06:19.800 --> 00:06:22.279
For any human being, you know, we are all
vulnerable.

00:06:22.279 --> 00:06:26.819
There is no vaccination against humanizing
attitudes.

00:06:26.819 --> 00:06:31.879
Is it is the term dehumanization relevant
to I'm thinking back a few months now during

00:06:31.879 --> 00:06:38.159
the coronavirus pandemic, the lieutenant governor
of Texas, Dan Patrick, gave this viral interview,

00:06:38.159 --> 00:06:44.229
I think it was on Fox News where he said something
about, listen, I would be willing to sacrifice

00:06:44.229 --> 00:06:48.849
myself, I think grandma or so to speak, something
like that.

00:06:48.849 --> 00:06:52.960
They're willing to sacrifice themselves for
the good of the economy by which he really

00:06:52.960 --> 00:06:54.490
meant the stock market.

00:06:54.490 --> 00:07:00.369
Is that a sort of dehumanization that we've
seen during coronavirus of when you see a

00:07:00.369 --> 00:07:05.550
number, 4000 died or 3000 died and you just
sat in your house and worked from home?

00:07:05.550 --> 00:07:10.419
It doesn't really feel that that difference
of a thousand, you don't feel it in any real

00:07:10.419 --> 00:07:11.419
way.

00:07:11.419 --> 00:07:12.639
It seems like that's a form of dehumanization.

00:07:12.639 --> 00:07:15.029
Some some people would call it demonization.

00:07:15.029 --> 00:07:19.759
I prefer a very fine grained notion of what
do you immunization is.

00:07:19.759 --> 00:07:21.629
So there are a bunch of related attitudes.

00:07:21.629 --> 00:07:28.860
But you see, I think if we're to understand
what I consider the most toxic form of these

00:07:28.860 --> 00:07:33.429
attitudes, which I reserve the term demonization
for, we need to get a good understanding of

00:07:33.429 --> 00:07:34.429
how it works.

00:07:34.429 --> 00:07:37.990
Otherwise we don't have a chance in hell of
dismantling it.

00:07:37.990 --> 00:07:38.990
Right.

00:07:38.990 --> 00:07:40.569
So I would call that callousness.

00:07:40.569 --> 00:07:45.339
And of course, what he's saying, in fact,
is he's willing to sacrifice the lives, the

00:07:45.339 --> 00:07:46.339
life of grandma.

00:07:46.339 --> 00:07:47.339
Right.

00:07:47.339 --> 00:07:49.039
Grandma and speaking their.

00:07:49.039 --> 00:07:53.759
Are are there things you've observed during
the pandemic that you would apply the term

00:07:53.759 --> 00:07:54.990
dehumanization to?

00:07:54.990 --> 00:07:56.559
Oh, yes, yes, yes.

00:07:56.559 --> 00:08:03.599
So what's gone on in this pandemic, as is
typical, by the way, of earlier pandemics,

00:08:03.599 --> 00:08:06.080
is xenophobia.

00:08:06.080 --> 00:08:12.400
So the pandemic is blamed on others, racial
others.

00:08:12.400 --> 00:08:17.669
Race and humanization are very closely tied
together.

00:08:17.669 --> 00:08:27.209
And then when you start talking about people
as disease, as dangerous outsiders, you are

00:08:27.209 --> 00:08:31.340
either well on the way towards humanization
or actually there.

00:08:31.340 --> 00:08:40.400
You see, it's it's not regarded as especially
acceptable to call people demons and monsters

00:08:40.400 --> 00:08:41.400
anymore.

00:08:41.400 --> 00:08:43.510
So we have substitute terms.

00:08:43.510 --> 00:08:49.720
They're diseased, they're criminal, they're
dangerous, they're bad to the bone.

00:08:49.720 --> 00:08:56.440
And what we get in this pandemic and in previous
pandemics is an upsurge in that kind of feeling,

00:08:56.440 --> 00:09:00.000
which has historically led to all sorts of
horrible things.

00:09:00.000 --> 00:09:06.260
It seems as though dehumanizing the rhetoric
of dehumanization probably has some specific

00:09:06.260 --> 00:09:11.070
overlap with authoritarian regimes and authoritarian
ism.

00:09:11.070 --> 00:09:13.680
Is it can we can you talk about that a little
bit?

00:09:13.680 --> 00:09:14.680
Yeah, sure.

00:09:14.680 --> 00:09:21.430
So there are two ways that I think the first
question to to address is why do immunization

00:09:21.430 --> 00:09:22.430
in the first place?

00:09:22.430 --> 00:09:30.090
And the reason, I believe, is that human beings
being highly social animals have difficulty.

00:09:30.090 --> 00:09:34.470
Performing acts of lethal or even sublethal
violence in one another, we're just equipped

00:09:34.470 --> 00:09:37.360
with these inhibitions.

00:09:37.360 --> 00:09:44.670
But those are very inconvenient if you want
to oppress and exterminate others.

00:09:44.670 --> 00:09:46.860
So.

00:09:46.860 --> 00:09:54.020
As I said, people in positions of power of
authority can tell us, you know, these others,

00:09:54.020 --> 00:09:57.360
they might look like you and me, they might
look like human beings.

00:09:57.360 --> 00:10:02.490
But really deep down, where it counts on the
inside, they're something else.

00:10:02.490 --> 00:10:09.080
And that is absolutely typical of authoritarian
going on fascist.

00:10:09.080 --> 00:10:16.140
It seems almost like a tool that's necessary
for such a regime to advance its its propaganda

00:10:16.140 --> 00:10:17.140
tool.

00:10:17.140 --> 00:10:19.240
And you talked about the role of propaganda
and dehumanization early.

00:10:19.240 --> 00:10:24.760
It seems that for for authoritarian regimes
that dehumanization of the others is necessary

00:10:24.760 --> 00:10:26.960
for them to advance their goals.

00:10:26.960 --> 00:10:29.120
It's certainly extremely helpful.

00:10:29.120 --> 00:10:31.440
I would say may maybe near necessary.

00:10:31.440 --> 00:10:38.470
I hesitate to say anything is necessary, but
it is certainly it is certainly helpful.

00:10:38.470 --> 00:10:43.710
Sometimes the dehumanizing ideology is distributed
through a community.

00:10:43.710 --> 00:10:48.590
So I as I say, in the first chapter of Honored
Humanity, I grew up in the Deep South in the

00:10:48.590 --> 00:10:53.500
50s and 60s and there dehumanizing attitudes
towards black people.

00:10:53.500 --> 00:10:55.080
They were that was in the air.

00:10:55.080 --> 00:11:01.050
You breathe, you need a Rush Limbaugh say
to propagate these things.

00:11:01.050 --> 00:11:05.050
What how casual was the dehumanization when
you were growing up in the South?

00:11:05.050 --> 00:11:13.140
Oh, it was just it just as you know, we would
say the sky is up and the earth is down.

00:11:13.140 --> 00:11:23.330
Black people who were not referred to by that
word, sure, the winner were seen as filthy.

00:11:23.330 --> 00:11:29.890
Subhuman animals, I mean, that was kids in
my high school would brag about hunting black

00:11:29.890 --> 00:11:33.420
kids with their B.B. guns on weekends for
sport.

00:11:33.420 --> 00:11:34.580
Hmm.

00:11:34.580 --> 00:11:41.540
That's that's and do you was the was there
a gradual transition out of that that aligned

00:11:41.540 --> 00:11:49.370
with the civil rights era 1964 Civil Rights
Act, did it lag that it was a gradual transition,

00:11:49.370 --> 00:11:50.380
but also retrenchment?

00:11:50.380 --> 00:11:54.160
I mean, these things have not gone away.

00:11:54.160 --> 00:11:57.620
Still, we like to think about the new south.

00:11:57.620 --> 00:12:03.150
But these are these things take a very, very,
very long time to change.

00:12:03.150 --> 00:12:05.160
They do not disappear quickly.

00:12:05.160 --> 00:12:09.280
They can remain latent, relatively invisible.

00:12:09.280 --> 00:12:15.180
For a long period of time, and then the social
environment changes a little bit.

00:12:15.180 --> 00:12:20.890
Someone in power like our former president
gives permission for certain kinds of attitudes

00:12:20.890 --> 00:12:25.260
and bang, you know, the Confederate flags
are out.

00:12:25.260 --> 00:12:31.130
What where where do things like eugenics and
phrenology play into this, because on the

00:12:31.130 --> 00:12:37.970
one hand, you know, stuff about measuring
skull ratios and all this stuff, it in a sense,

00:12:37.970 --> 00:12:43.790
it's very human in that we're looking at the
minutia of of the characteristics of the human

00:12:43.790 --> 00:12:44.790
form.

00:12:44.790 --> 00:12:48.000
But it's its use seems to be dehumanizing.

00:12:48.000 --> 00:12:51.070
Well, it's actually a bit worse than that.

00:12:51.070 --> 00:12:55.480
So if we look at the history of scientific
racism in the United States.

00:12:55.480 --> 00:12:56.480
Yeah.

00:12:56.480 --> 00:13:01.650
During the period when these practices were
beginning to flourish, eugenics is a bit later.

00:13:01.650 --> 00:13:05.310
But certainly the skull measurement work.

00:13:05.310 --> 00:13:14.570
The premier scientific authorities in the
country described black people as literally

00:13:14.570 --> 00:13:16.480
another species.

00:13:16.480 --> 00:13:23.180
They were learned papers on this and mixed
race.

00:13:23.180 --> 00:13:28.000
People were seen as literally as hybrids between
different species.

00:13:28.000 --> 00:13:34.170
So these see you see, they're the dehumanizing
attitude is endorsed by scientific authority.

00:13:34.170 --> 00:13:35.190
It could be scientific authority.

00:13:35.190 --> 00:13:40.400
It could be political or it's anyone who has
that kind of influence, right?

00:13:40.400 --> 00:13:41.400
Yeah.

00:13:41.400 --> 00:13:43.690
I mean, that that makes and I know you write
about this in the book.

00:13:43.690 --> 00:13:48.470
It brings me to the topic of climate change
and climate science.

00:13:48.470 --> 00:13:50.880
What do you say maybe to the audience?

00:13:50.880 --> 00:13:55.490
It's not obvious the connection between dehumanization,
the way we've talked about in authoritarian

00:13:55.490 --> 00:13:59.510
regimes and so on, and the connection to climate
change.

00:13:59.510 --> 00:14:06.790
Yeah, well, the the aspect of climate change
that's most salient for me and my work on

00:14:06.790 --> 00:14:12.630
the organization is that I think catastrophic
climate change, which I don't think anyone's

00:14:12.630 --> 00:14:13.870
going to do anything about.

00:14:13.870 --> 00:14:16.410
I think we're well, too late for that.

00:14:16.410 --> 00:14:24.040
And there's insufficient motivation, despite
the fine words that are said is going to create

00:14:24.040 --> 00:14:32.710
a situation which is a perfect storm for really
dangerous forms of demonization.

00:14:32.710 --> 00:14:39.310
There will be refugee problems like the world
has never known, you know, when when the population

00:14:39.310 --> 00:14:46.120
of Bangladesh spills into India.

00:14:46.120 --> 00:14:51.760
And resources run short and infrastructures
break down.

00:14:51.760 --> 00:15:03.540
This is going to generate intense xenophobia
and I would put good money on demonizing rhetoric

00:15:03.540 --> 00:15:09.320
of the worst, most genocidal sort is the only
way to prevent it to prevent the climate refugees

00:15:09.320 --> 00:15:10.740
from becoming refugees.

00:15:10.740 --> 00:15:16.420
I mean, is there is if that happens, is there
really any way to prevent what you're describing?

00:15:16.420 --> 00:15:21.760
I'd like to know and I'd like to I'd like
to think that there is yeah, we're human beings.

00:15:21.760 --> 00:15:25.911
We can we can change our behavior, but we
have to know about it.

00:15:25.911 --> 00:15:27.040
We have to be aware of it.

00:15:27.040 --> 00:15:32.160
We have to be vigilant and there have to be
policies in place to help us.

00:15:32.160 --> 00:15:40.890
And I just don't think we're awake enough
to the possibilities to take the sorts of

00:15:40.890 --> 00:15:42.410
steps which will be necessary.

00:15:42.410 --> 00:15:45.940
That's one reason I wrote the book.

00:15:45.940 --> 00:15:51.310
Demonization is an ongoing problem and.

00:15:51.310 --> 00:15:57.200
To do something about it, we have to understand
it, we have to understand how it works, why

00:15:57.200 --> 00:16:04.089
we are all vulnerable to humanizing propaganda
and what steps we might be able to take to

00:16:04.089 --> 00:16:07.070
at least constrain it.

00:16:07.070 --> 00:16:12.160
The book is on inhumanity, dehumanization
and how to resist it.

00:16:12.160 --> 00:16:17.060
We've been speaking with the book's author,
Professor David Livingstone Smith.

00:16:17.060 --> 00:16:19.290
An important topic.

00:16:19.290 --> 00:16:21.280
And I appreciate you joining me to talk about
it today.

00:16:21.280 --> 00:16:23.470
I appreciate you inviting The David Pakman
Show and David Pakman Dutkiewicz.

